<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Repository-scoped NuGet configuration.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    Central Package Management (Directory.Packages.props) refuses to restore ambiguously when
    more than one package source is configured, because it cannot know which feed a given
    package is supposed to come from (NU1507). On this machine two sources were inherited from
    the machine-level configuration: nuget.org and dynamics365-commerce - the second belonging
    to an unrelated project.

    <clear /> drops every inherited source, so this repository restores from exactly the feeds
    named below regardless of what is configured machine-wide or on a build agent. Other
    projects on this machine are unaffected; this file applies only to this directory tree.

    WHY THIS IS A SECURITY CONTROL, NOT JUST A BUILD FIX
    Multiple unconstrained feeds is the precondition for a dependency-confusion attack: a
    package published to a secondary feed under a name this solution references can be resolved
    in preference to the intended one. Package source mapping below states that every package
    resolves from nuget.org and nowhere else. See docs/SECURITY.md, "Supply chain".

    ADDING A PRIVATE FEED LATER
    Add the source AND an explicit mapping pattern for it - never a bare '*' on a second feed,
    which reopens exactly the hole this closes. Credentials for a private feed belong in
    environment variables or the CI secret store, never in this file.
  -->

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <packageSourceMapping>
    <packageSource key="nuget.org">
      <package pattern="*" />
    </packageSource>
  </packageSourceMapping>

  <packageRestore>
    <add key="enabled" value="True" />
    <add key="automatic" value="True" />
  </packageRestore>

</configuration>
